{
  "id": "Compound::DB00445 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Epirubicin is implicated in the pathogenesis of hematologic cancer through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Epirubicin, through its interaction with Doxorubicin, contributes to the development of hematologic conditions such as myelodysplasia and urethral obstruction, which in turn may influence the progression of hematologic cancer. The shared lowest common ancestor of Epirubicin and Doxorubicin, both classified under the category of anthracycline antineoplastic antibiotics, indicates a common pharmacological mechanism. This classification suggests that Epirubicin, as an anthracycline antibiotic, may exert its effects through similar molecular pathways as Doxorubicin, potentially leading to the observed clinical outcomes. The consistent association between Epirubicin and hematologic cancer, as indicated by the shared causal relationships and the common pharmacological class, supports the hypothesis that Epirubicin may contribute to the development of hematologic malignancies through its antineoplastic properties. The evidence highlights the importance of considering the broader pharmacological context of anthracycline antibiotics in understanding their role in disease progression.",
